\DOC prove_coinductive_relations_exist

\TYPE {prove_coinductive_relations_exist : term -> thm}

\SYNOPSIS
Prove existence of coinductively defined relations without defining them.

\DESCRIBE
The function {prove_coinductive_relations_exist} should be given a
specification for a coinductively defined relation {R}, or more generally a
family {R1,...,Rn} of mutually coinductive relations, in the same format as for
{new_coinductive_definition}. It returns an existential theorem
{A |- ?R1 ... Rn. rules /\ coinduction /\ cases}, where {rules},
{coinduction} and {cases} are the rule, coinduction and cases theorems,
explained further in the entry for {new_coinductive_definition}. In contrast
with {new_coinductive_definition}, no actual definitions are made. The
assumption list {A} is normally empty, but will include any monotonicity
hypotheses that were not proven automatically.

\FAILURE
Fails if the form of the rules is wrong.

\EXAMPLE
Infinite paths in a relation:
{
  # prove_coinductive_relations_exist
     `!x:A y. R x y /\ infpath R y ==> infpath R x`;;
  val it : thm =
    |- ?infpath.
           !R.
               (!x y. R x y /\ infpath R y ==> infpath R x) /\
               (!infpath'.
                    (!a. infpath' a ==> (?y. R a y /\ infpath' y))
                    ==> (!a. infpath' a ==> infpath R a)) /\
               (!a. infpath R a <=> (?y. R a y /\ infpath R y))
}
\noindent and an example where monotonicity does not hold, so the theorem has
a hypothesis:
{
  # prove_coinductive_relations_exist `!x. ~P(x) ==> P(x+1)`;;
  val it : thm =
    !P P'.
        (!a. P a ==> P' a)
        ==> (!a. (?x. a = x + 1 /\ ~P x) ==> (?x. a = x + 1 /\ ~P' x))
    |- ?P. (!x. ~P x ==> P (x + 1)) /\
           (!P'. (!a. P' a ==> (?x. a = x + 1 /\ ~P' x))
                 ==> (!a. P' a ==> P a)) /\
           (!a. P a <=> (?x. a = x + 1 /\ ~P x))
}

\USES
Using existence of coinductive relations as an auxiliary device inside a
proof.

\SEEALSO
derive_strong_coinduction, new_coinductive_definition,
prove_inductive_relations_exist, prove_monotonicity_hyps.

\ENDDOC
